<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Koxinga</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1624">1624</a>–<a href="/years/1662">1662</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Koxinga, born Zheng Chenggong, was a seventeenth-century Ming loyalist commander who resisted the Qing conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from bases on the south-eastern coast. In 1662 he expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> after besieging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-zeelandia-taiwan">Fort Zeelandia</a>. The loss of Taiwan was a notable setback for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koxinga.jpg" alt="Koxinga" width="251" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Portrait_of_Koxinga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zheng Chenggong, born Zheng Sen, was born on 27 August 1624 at Hirado in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-zhilong">Zheng Zhilong</a>, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> merchant from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>, and a Japanese woman known by her surname Tagawa. He lived there until the age of seven, then moved to Fujian. He became a xiucai in the imperial examination in 1638 and in 1644 studied at the Guozijian under the scholar Qian Qianyi. In 1645 the Prince of Tang was installed as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/longwu-emperor">Longwu Emperor</a> with the support of Zheng Zhilong, and gave Zheng Sen the name Chenggong and the title Koxinga, meaning lord granted the imperial surname.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/longwu-emperor">Longwu Emperor</a> was captured by the Qing and executed in October 1646. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-zhilong">Zheng Zhilong</a> surrendered on 21 November 1646, against his family's objections, and Koxinga and his uncles succeeded to the leadership of his forces. Operating outside Xiamen, he used naval superiority for amphibious raids in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>. By 1650 he led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-family">Zheng family</a> and pledged allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongli-emperor">Yongli Emperor</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a>, who made him Prince of Yanping in 1655. Talks with the Qing lasted until November 1654 and failed. On 9 May 1656 a Qing fleet attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kinmen">Kinmen</a> and, partly through a storm, lost most of its ships. He later besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> for almost three weeks from 24 August, but was defeated and slipped back to his ships.</p><p>In 1661 Koxinga landed to attack Dutch Formosa, helped by Taiwanese Aboriginal groups who turned against the Dutch. The governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-coyett">Frederick Coyett</a> surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-zeelandia-taiwan">Fort Zeelandia</a> on 1 February 1662, ending 38 years of Dutch rule on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a>. During the siege Koxinga executed the missionary Antonius Hambroek and took his teenage daughter as a concubine. He then set about making Taiwan a base for Ming loyalists.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1662 he sent the friar Vittorio Riccio to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a> to demand tribute from the Spanish, who refused; the planned attack never took place. Koxinga died in June 1662, aged 37, of an unspecified illness; modern historians suspect malaria. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zheng-jing">Zheng Jing</a> succeeded him as Prince of Yanping, and a dynastic state on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> continued until 1683. Koxinga is worshipped as a god in coastal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> and Taiwan, where a temple in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tainan">Tainan</a> honours him and his mother.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Koxinga” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Dutch overseas expansion faltered after the loss of Taiwan to <b>Koxinga</b>'s forces in 1662 and of New Netherland and New Amsterdam to England in 1664, and the Fourth Anglo-Dutch War of 1780–1784 left both the VOC and the GWC mortally wounded.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1662 the Ming loyalist Zheng Chenggong, known in the West as <b>Koxinga</b>, forced the Dutch East India Company to surrender its fortress on Taiwan.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1661 the Ming loyalist <b>Koxinga</b> landed with a large fleet and besieged Fort Zeelandia for nine months.</blockquote><a href="/state/dutch-formosa">Dutch Formosa</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Koxinga</b> (Zheng Chenggong), son of the merchant-admiral Zheng Zhilong, led Ming loyalist forces on the Fujian coast after 1646.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-tungning">Kingdom of Tungning</a></li><li><blockquote>The Qing order to shave the forehead in 1645 drove many to resist, and former rebels such as Li Dingguo and the coastal power of <b>Koxinga</b> fought for the Ming cause.</blockquote><a href="/state/southern-ming">Southern Ming</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hirado. Died in the Kingdom of Tungning. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.32,129.49&amp;year=1648" aria-label="Hirado on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Koxinga"><image href="/maps/globes/g120n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5675 4689h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5675 4689h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.32,129.49&amp;year=1648">What was here in 1648? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
